Z||000000||overlord||09-04-2000||09:17 AM||||the past few months i noticed an increasinlgy vibration only when i am braking at slow speeds.<BR>I dont know anything about brakes at all and have never changed them or looked at em since i bought this car.<P>The vibration makes the whole car "wiggle".<BR>Please say its something easy to fix if any ideas.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||09-04-2000||10:17 AM||||Sounds like your rotor(s) are warped...<P>This is a fairly common occurrence on GM cars. Another possibility is a brake pad with a foreign substance such as grease or brake fluid (a leaky caliper can do this) on it which grips intermittently.<P>The warped rotor is the most likely offender. Figuring which one (or which ones) is warped can be tough. It's likely that whichever rotors are warped are worn to the point where they can't be resurfaced.<P>Don't be afraid of tackling a brake job if you haven't done it before. The Chilton and Haynes manuals detail the job pretty well, and other than the Torx bit you need to remove the calipers from the car (T50, I believe), no special tools are needed. <P>As to rotors, a lot of times you can look at them and tell they're bad. Rotors that have been overheated are often discolored, and wear often results in deep grooves in the rotor's braking surfaces.<P>My first four wheel brake job was on my 2m4, and I didn't experience any serious problems other than hard to loosen bolts and frozen emergency brake cables.<P>So go for it, and save yourself some money. Do it once and you'll know how for life.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||09-04-2000||10:28 AM||||A shimmy or wobble has meant to me, a warped rotor.  However when this has happened to me in the past (non Fiero cars) it happened at all speeds, worst at harder braking at faster speeds.  It may be loose calipers or uneven braking at the wheels.  Does the car pull?<P>Since I don't work on my Fiero either, I'd get it looked at at your "trusted" Fiero mechanic. A warped rotor can be re ground smooth if there is enough material on the disc.  Loose calipers can be adjusted.  Depending on milage and condition of the brakes, age of the car etc, you may be in for a big repair bill.  And if you have an 88, it'll be more.  You want to be able to stop your Fiero.  Reading through all the threads on problems many of the do it yourself brake fixers are having with the brake repair, I won't think about trying to fix the brakes myself.<P>No easy answer until the cause is found.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-04-2000||05:17 PM||||T45 for the caliper bolts.||||reg||1
